## 1 Introduction

Gamma-ray bursts (GRBs), the most luminous electromagnetic explosions in the Universe, are traditionally classified into two categories based on their duration: short-duration GRBs (SGRBs; T_{90}<2s) and long-duration GRBs (LGRBs; T_{90}>2s) (Kouveliotou et al., 1993). Robust associations of the underlying supernovae (SNe) with some LGRBs suggest that they are likely related to the collapse of massive stars (Woosley, 1993; Galama et al., 1998; Stanek et al., 2003; Fruchter et al., 2006; Pian et al., 2006; Woosley & Bloom, 2006). In contrast, some SGRBs are associated with nearby host galaxies with little star formation, and others do not have an associated SN (Berger et al., 2005; Fox et al., 2005; Hjorth et al., 2005; Tanvir et al., 2005), suggesting that the SGRBs are possibly originated from the merger of binary compact stars, such as binary neutron star (BNS) mergers (Paczynski, 1986; Eichler et al., 1989) and neutron star-black hole (NS-BH) mergers (Paczynski, 1991). On the other hand, such binary systems are also considered to be potential sources of gravitational-wave radiation and kilonova emission (Berger, 2014; Zhang, 2018). The first "smoking gun" evidence to confirm the BNS merger as the source of SGRBs, gravitational-wave radiation, and kilonova, emerged from the direct detection of GW 170817 via Advanced LIGO and Virgo, which was associated with electromagnetic counterparts, such as GRB 170817A and AT 2017gfo (Abbott et al., 2017; Alexander et al., 2017; Arcavi et al., 2017; Chornock et al., 2017; Coulter et al., 2017; Covino et al., 2017; Drout et al., 2017; Evans et al., 2017; Goldstein et al., 2017; Kasen et al., 2017; Kasliwal et al., 2017; Pian et al., 2017; Savchenko et al., 2017; Shappee et al., 2017; Smartt et al., 2017; Tanaka et al., 2017; Tanvir et al., 2017; Zhang et al., 2018).

Except for the confirmed event GW 170817/ GRB 170817A/ AT 2017gfo, some SGRBs have been associated with kilonova candidates, such as GRB 050709 (Jin et al., 2016), GRB 130603B (Berger et al., 2013; Tanvir et al., 2013) and GRB 160821B (Lu et al., 2017; Lamb et al., 2019; Troja et al., 2019). However, from an observational point of view, it is not only SGRBs that exhibit the features of the merger but also several LGRBs, which are more likely SGRBs with extended emission (EE-SGRB), showing potential kilonova signatures but lacking an associated SN, for example, GRB 060614 (Della Valle et al., 2006; Fynbo et al., 2006; Gal-Yam et al., 2006; Gehrels et al., 2006; Metzger et al., 2008; Yang et al., 2015), GRB 211211A (Rastinejad et al., 2022; Troja et al., 2022; Yang et al., 2022; Chang et al., 2023; Gompertz et al., 2023), GRB 211227A (Lu et al., 2022; Ferro et al., 2023), and GRB 230307A (Gillanders et al., 2023; Du et al., 2024; Levan et al., 2024; Yang et al., 2024; Sun et al., 2025). On the other hand, the short GRB 200826A, which seems to be associated with an SN, suggests an origin from the collapse of massive stars (Ahumada et al., 2021; Zhang et al., 2021). In summary, the traditional long-versus-short-GRB scheme no longer always corresponds to the two distinct physical origins (Zhang, 2006; Lu et al., 2010; Berger et al., 2013; Lu et al., 2014).

One interesting question focuses on the EE-SGRBs; it is challenging for the BNS mergers and NS-BH mergers to explain the duration of their prompt \\gamma-ray emission due to their shorter timescales of the mergers. Thus, the neutron star-white dwarf (NS-WD) merger has been proposed to explain the observational properties of EE-SGRBs (Yang et al., 2022; Zhong et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2024). The reason is that WDs are less dense than NSs, and the free-fall timescale of NS-WD mergers could be significantly longer than that of BNS mergers or NS-BH mergers. If this is the case, it could naturally explain the long timescale characteristics of these EE-SGRBs. However, WDs are proton rich, so the ejecta from the NS-WD mergers may not be as neutron rich as those from systems like NS-NS mergers, NS-BH mergers, colapsars, etc. Therefore, the astrophysical environment of the NS-WD mergers seems not to be suitable for r-process element production (Fernandez et al., 2019; Bobrick et al., 2022; Kaltenborn et al., 2023).

To understand the process of NS-WD mergers, we provide a relatively clear picture of NS-WD mergers: NS-WD mergers are originated from binaries formed through common envelope evolution and gravitational-wave-driven orbital decay. Population synthesis suggests that such systems are abundant in the Galaxy (Nelemans et al., 2001). When unstable mass transfer begins, the WD is tidally disrupted to form an accretion disk around the NS (Papaloizou and Pringle, 1984; Fryer et al., 1999). Numerical simulations have extensively explored the subsequent disk evolution, which involves nuclear burning, fallback accretion, angular momentum transport, and outflows driven by neutrino cooling or magnetic processes. These features are somewhat similar to that of found in postmerger disks from NS-NS systems (Fernandez and Metzger, 2013). Recent works have further investigated how the structure, composition, and thermodynamic state of the disk affect the ejecta mass, nucleosynthesis, and the properties of the resulting faint and rapidly evolving transients (Zenati et al., 2019, 2020; Bobrick et al., 2022; Kaltenborn et al., 2023). Especially, Chen and Liang (2024) provide insight into the radioactive heating of heavy elements from nuclear burning, which may inform further studies of the NS-WD merger ejecta.

It is interesting to ask the following two questions: What is the approximate mass number of the heaviest element that can be produced by NS-WD mergers? How bright is the kilonova-like emission corresponding to it?

In this work, we investigate the heavier elements produced by NS-WD mergers based on nucleosynthesis simulations. Then, we explore the luminosity of the corresponding kilonova-like emission produced by the radioactive decay of the elements produced by nucleosynthesis based on detailed numerical calculations. Finally, we compare the calculated luminosity of the kilonov-like emission with the observations of the possible kilonova associated with GRB 211211A. This Letter is organized as follows. In Section 2, we introduce the details of nucleosynthesis and the method we adopted to calculate the kilonova-like light curves. The results of the nucleosynthesis elements and the corresponding kilonov-like light curves are presented in Section 3. The application to a possible kilonova emission associated with GRB 211211A based on our results is shown in Section 4. Conclusions are drawn in Section 5, along with some additional discussion. Throughout this Letter, we adopt a concordance cosmology with the parameters H_{0}=71 km s{}^{-1} Mpc{}^{-1}, \\Omega_{\\rm M}=0.30, and \\Omega_{\\Lambda}=0.70.

## 2 Methods

In general, based on the mass of white dwarfs (M_{\\rm WD}) in WD-NS binaries, there are two main evolutionary pathways that are widely discussed for such binary systems, namely stable mass transfer (M_{\\rm WD}<M_{\\rm WD,c}) and unstable mass transfer (M_{\\rm WD}>M_{\\rm WD,c}), where M_{\\rm WD,c}\\sim 0.2M_{\\odot} is the critical mass of the WD (Margalit and Metzger, 2016). More than 20 WD-NS binaries have been discovered in our Galaxy, and the mass of WDs in such binaries is larger than the critical mass (O'Shaughnessy and Kim, 2010). It suggests that the majority of WD-NS binary systems will undergo a merger with the process of unstable mass transfer within a Hubble time (Toonen et al., 2018). In our work, we adopt the unstable mass transfer model and select three different groups of WD-NS systems for simulation, with mass groups of (0.75-1.4) M_{\\odot}, (1-1.25) M_{\\odot}, and (1-1.8) M_{\\odot}which we named as Model-A, Model-B, and Model-C, respectively (see Table 1).

### Nucleosynthesis

The merger of binary compact stars (e.g., NS-NS, NS-BH) or SN explosions is an important site for the nucleosynthesis of heavy elements. One interesting question is how heavy the heaviest element can be formed during the WD-NS mergers. In this section, we perform the nucleosynthesis via the nuclear reaction network code SkyNet to do the numerical simulations (Lippuner and Roberts, 2015, 2017).

SkyNet can compute the evolution of nucleosynthesis in a wide range of astrophysical scenarios where nucleosynthesis occurs, and it has been widely used to evolve the abundance of nuclear species under the influence of nuclear reactions. The network contains 7843 nuclides and 140,000 reactions, ranging from free neutrons (Z=0) and protons (Z=1) to {}^{337}Cn (Z=112). SkyNet adopts a modified version of the Helmholtz equation of state (EOS; Timmes and Swesty (2000)) to calculate the entropy. The nuclear reaction rates used in SkyNet are taken from the JINA REACLIB database (Cyburt et al., 2010). Nuclear masses and partition functions are included in the WebNucleo XML file distributed with REACLIB for nuclide species that are without experimental data, and we use the theoretical data derived from the finite-range droplet model (Moller et al., 2015).

The first step is to evolve the accretion disk, which is composed of material generated by the tidal disruption of the WD. The initial density profile of the disk that we adopt can be described in Margalit and Metzger (2016), and we adopt the average density as the initial condition density in our simulations. In terms of the temperature, we investigate the effect of different initial temperatures due to the poorly known temperature. Thus, three groups of temperatures, such as 4, 5, and 6 GK, are used in the simulations. We initialize the composition of the material in the disk, namely, equal parts of carbon and oxygen, and with 1% helium. During the disk evolution, we assume that the overall density and temperature of the whole disk do not change too much, and we adopt a constant density and temperature trajectory in SkyNet to perform the simulations. The total timescale of disk evolution is set to 60 s, and one can obtain the final abundance of the disk evolution.

Then, we set the final abundance of the disk evolution as the initial abundance of the ejecta evolution. In our nucleosynthesis simulations of the ejecta, for the density profile, we use the parameterized trajectory provided by Lippuner and Roberts (2015). The initial density of the ejecta is set to \\rho_{\\rm ej}=3.00\\times 10^{5} g cm{}^{-3} for Model-A, which is taken from the results of Zenati et al. (2019). By assuming that the ejecta of Model-B and Model-C share the same radius as in Model-A, together with the results of numerical relativity simulations given by Kaltenborn et al. (2023), one can derive the initial densities of Model-B and Model-C to be 4.42 \\times 10{}^{5} g cm{}^{-3} and 3.68 \\times 10{}^{5} g cm{}^{-3}, respectively. Table 1 shows the details of the initial parameter settings for both disk and ejecta. Moreover, the expansion timescale is approximately set to be 3 s, considering the long duration of WD-NS mergers. For ejecta evolution, we also set three groups of initial temperatures, which are 4, 5, and 6 GK, respectively. Then, the temperature profiles are set as TV^{1/3}= const for the evolution of the ejecta, which is because the expansion of the ejecta is nearly adiabatic (Wollaeger et al., 2018; Farouqi et al., 2010).

### Radioactive heating rate

A large number of elements can be produced through nucleosynthesis, releasing a substantial amount of energy through radioactive decay. The energy from the radioactive decay of these elements can heat ejecta through interactions. So, the total effective heating rate of the merger ejecta contains the sum of the contributions from all radioactive decay of elements, and it can be expressed as

\\dot{Q}(t)=f_{\\rm tot}(t)\\dot{q}(t). 

Here, \\dot{q}(t) is the radioactive heating rate for all nuclides, which can be directly calculated by SkyNet. f_{\\rm tot}(t) is the thermalization efficiency which is estimated by the analytic method proposed in Barnes et al. (2016),

f_{\\rm tot}(t)=0.36\\bigg{[}\\exp(-at)+\\frac{\\ln(1+2bt^{d})}{2bt^{d}}\\bigg{]},  where a, b, and d are constants. In our simulations and calculations, we fixed the values of a=0.27, b=0.10 and d=0.60(Barnes et al., 2016).

### Kilonova-like emission

The kilonova emission, which is an optical/infrared transient, is generated in the ejected material and is powered by the radioactive decay of r-process nuclei (Li and Paczynski, 1998; Metzger et al., 2010; Yang et al., 2015; Ma et al., 2018). If extra energy is injected into such a transient, except for radioactive decay of r-process nuclei, the kilonova will be brighter and is called mergernova (Yu et al., 2013; Gao et al., 2017; Yuan et al., 2021). In our work, the merger ejecta is assumed to be spherically symmetric (Kasen et al., 2017); even some numerical simulations of double NS merger suggest that the ejecta or radioactive decay of r-process is more or less anisotropic (Ciolfi et al., 2017; Siegel and Metzger, 2018; Collins et al., 2023). By adopting an average velocity of ejecta v_{\\rm ej}=0.1 c taken from the simulation result of Zenati et al. (2019), and following the method in Chen and Liang (2024), we divide the merger ejecta into n layers and treat the evolution of each layer independently. The velocities of the innermost layer (first layer) and the outermost layer (last layer) are set to v_{\\rm min}=v_{\\rm ej}-0.03c and v_{\\rm max}=v_{\\rm ej}+0.03c, respectively. Thus, the expansion velocity of the ith layer is given by

v_{i}=v_{\\rm min}+(i-1)\\frac{v_{\\rm max}-v_{\\rm min}}{n-1}. 

The radius of the ith layer at time t is given by R_{i}=v_{i}t, and the mass of the ith layer is m_{i}=\\int_{R_{i}}^{R_{i+1}}4\\pi\\rho(v_{i},t)r^{2}dr. The density profile is assumed to be described as a broken power-law function, as adopted in Kasen et al. (2017), namely,

\\rho(v_{i},t)=\\begin{cases}\\zeta_{\\rho}\\frac{M_{\\rm ej}}{v_{\\rm t}^{3}t}(\\frac {v_{\\rm t}}{v_{\\rm t}})^{-\\delta},&v_{i}<v_{\\rm t},\\\\ \\zeta_{\\rho}\\frac{M_{\\rm ej}}{v_{\\rm t}^{3}t}(\\frac{v_{\\rm t}}{v_{\\rm t}})^{-n },&v_{i}\\geq v_{\\rm t}.\\end{cases} 

Here, the default values for the exponents are \\delta=1 and n=10. The transition velocity v_{\\rm t} between the inner and outer layers is given by

v_{\\rm t}=\\zeta_{v}(\\frac{2E}{M_{\\rm ej}})^{1/2},  where E=\\sum_{i}m_{i}v_{i}^{2}/2 is the sum of the kinetic energy of all layers, \\zeta_{\\rho} and \\zeta_{v} are the normalization constants. Based on the mass conservation for a given M_{\\rm ej}=\\int_{R_{i}}^{R_{n}}4\\pi\\rho(v_{i},t)r^{2}dr, \\zeta_{\\rho} and v_{\\rm t} can be derived.

The main difference between the model we used and the toy model presented in Metzger (2017, 2019) is that the rate of density decreasing for different mass layers in the ejecta is different. The densities of different mass layers in Metzger (2019) are all decreasing at the same rate. However, in our model, we adopt the density profile used in Kasen et al. (2017), i.e., the densities of different layers have different decreasing rates. The density of the inner layers decreases more slowly, while the density of the outer layers decreases faster. Within the scenario of the NS and WD merger, because of the continuous injection of material into the accretion disk (and the long duration of the process), the density profile we used should be more suitable.

Following the method in Metzger (2019), the evolution of thermal energy of each layer can be written as

\\frac{dE_{i}}{dt}=-\\frac{E_{i}}{R_{i}}\\frac{dR_{i}}{dt}-L_{i}+\\dot{Q}(t)m_{i},  where E_{i} is the internal energy of the ith layer and \\dot{Q}(t) is the heating rate, which can be given by Equation (1). L_{i} is the radiation luminosity of each layer, which can be estimated as

L_{i}=\\frac{E_{i}}{t_{d,i}+t_{lc,i}},  where t_{d,i}=\\tau_{i}v_{i}t/c is the photon diffusion timescale and t_{lc,i}=v_{i}t/c is the energy loss time limitation to the light-crossing time. The optical depth (\\tau_{i}) of the ith shell is given by

\\tau_{i}=\\int_{R_{i}}^{\\infty}\\rho(v_{i},t)\\kappa dr,  where \\kappa is the opacity of the ejecta. In our calculation, the ejecta are predominantly composed of iron-group elements; thus, we set \\kappa=0.2cm{}^{2}g{}^{-1}, which is a reasonable approximation for iron-rich ejecta in Metzger (2019). The total bolometric luminosity is the sum of the contributions from all layers,

L_{\\rm bol}=\\sum_{i}L_{i}. 

The effective temperature of the thermal emission is written as

T_{\\rm eff}=\\Big{(}\\frac{L_{\\rm bol}}{4\\pi\\sigma_{\\rm SB}R_{\\rm ph}^{2}}\\Big{)} ^{1/4},  where \\sigma_{\\rm SB} is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant and R_{\\rm ph} is the photospheric radius when the optical depth equals unity, i.e.,

\\tau_{\\rm ph}=\\int_{R_{\\rm ph}}^{\\infty}\\rho(v_{i},t)\\kappa dr=1. 

